Attack 4 — seed-derivation identity between the optimizer's internal simulator
(runGuardrailOptimizerScenarios) and the test's direct comparison call
(analysis.MonteCarloWithResults -> runSimulations): both derive per-run seeds via
`master := rand.New(rand.NewSource(baseSeed)); seeds[i] = master.Int63()` then
`rand.New(rand.NewSource(seeds[i]))` per run — byte-identical derivation. The two
paths differ only in config.MinMonthlySpendingReal/CaptureSpendingYears (floor
tracking); traced the sim loop (monte_carlo.go): the depletion decision
(`Survives: !depleted`) is fixed at the depletion month, and the only effect of a
non-nil floorTracker is to keep the per-run loop running (consuming further RNG
draws) *after* depletion instead of breaking early — draws that cannot retroactively
change an already-fixed `depleted` flag. So DepletionPaths equality is not
accidental one-shot luck; it's structurally guaranteed by RunSingleMonteCarloSimulation's
control flow, and the oracle (independent fixture, independent seeds) confirms it: see
below, exact-count equality held on 9 distinct candidates across two independent test
files with different seeds/fixtures.

